# LLMForge · Render Router — environment
#
# Entry point that audits this device in the browser (WebGL2, GPU renderer,
# memory, CPU threads, stress benchmark) and redirects to either the Heavy UI
# (UI/, full WebGL) or the Lite UI (UI_lite/, canvas-2D). All three apps run on
# the same machine and bind 0.0.0.0, so the router reaches them by hostname +
# port — it never proxies content, it only hands the browser off.

# Server
# HOSTNAME="0.0.0.0"
# PORT=3002

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Routing to the UIs (Tailscale)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The heavy UI runs on prod :3000 and the lite UI on prod :3001. From anywhere
# on the tailnet these are reachable by MagicDNS hostname + port:
#
#   http://pratic-battleaxb450mkm2.tail5e5151.ts.net:3000   (Heavy UI)
#   http://pratic-battleaxb450mkm2.tail5e5151.ts.net:3001   (Lite UI)
#
# Set NEXT_PUBLIC_HEAVY_UI_URL / NEXT_PUBLIC_LITE_UI_URL to the exact URLs the
# router should send browsers to. These are NEXT_PUBLIC_* — they are inlined at
# BUILD time, so rebuild the router after changing them.
#
# When both are left unset, the router derives its targets from wherever it was
# itself opened (localhost, a LAN IP, or the tailnet hostname) using the
# NODE_ENV-appropriate ports: prod → 3000/3001, dev → 3210/3211. In local dev
# against the dev servers, comment the two lines below out so the router
# follows the dev ports instead (heavy :3210, lite :3211).
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HEAVY_UI_URL="https://pratic-battleaxb450mkm2.tail5e5151.ts.net/heavy"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LITE_UI_URL="https://pratic-battleaxb450mkm2.tail5e5151.ts.net/lite"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exposing everything publicly (Tailscale Funnel / serve)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The router is a redirecting entry point and never needs its own TLS. To make
# the whole stack reachable on the public HTTPS URL (https://host.ts.net),
# map paths with `tailscale serve`, e.g.:
#
#   sudo tailscale serve --https=443 --set-path=/ --bg 127.0.0.1:3002   # router
#   sudo tailscale serve --https=443 --set-path=/heavy --bg 127.0.0.1:3000
#   sudo tailscale serve --https=443 --set-path=/lite  --bg 127.0.0.1:3001
#
# then point the router's NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars at the public paths:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_HEAVY_UI_URL="https://host.ts.net/heavy"
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_LITE_UI_URL="https://host.ts.net/lite"
#
# The UI apps' own NEXTAUTH_URL / AUTH_URL must match whatever host a user
# actually reaches them from — see UI/.env.example for those requirements.
